(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-infective-endocarditis-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Infective Endocarditis Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"In people who inject drugs, what share of tricuspid valve endocarditis is caused by Staphylococcus aureus?","choices":["Under 5%","10 to 20%","30 to 40%","80 to 90%"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In people who inject drugs, Staphylococcus aureus causes more than 60% of all endocarditis and 80-90% of cases where the tricuspid valve is infected. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which disorder in the differential of infective endocarditis causes myalgia with elevated inflammatory markers?","choices":["Polymyalgia rheumatica","Antiphospholipid syndrome","Atrial myxoma","Tuberculosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Polymyalgia rheumatica presents with myalgia and elevated inflammatory markers (erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein), mimicking the constitutional picture of endocarditis. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What is the most likely complication of fungal infective endocarditis?","choices":["Isolated first-degree heart block","Spontaneous resolution within days","Painful nodules on the finger pads","Large peripheral artery emboli"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In fungal endocarditis the most likely complication is embolism and occlusion of larger peripheral arteries from embolization. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"In a high-risk patient, which oral event does not call for endocarditis prophylaxis?","choices":["Bleeding from lip trauma","Periodontal scaling","A tooth extraction","A gum biopsy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Bleeding from trauma to the lips or oral mucosa is on the list that does not require prophylaxis, with dental radiographs and orthodontic adjustments. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A perivalvular abscess that extends into the upper ventricular septum causes which complication?","choices":["Rupture of a mycotic aneurysm","Septic emboli to the lungs","Interruption of the conduction system","Pericarditis from epicardial spread"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. When the abscess extends into the upper ventricular septum it can interrupt the conduction system; extension into the epicardium causes pericarditis. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"The HACEK (Haemophilus, Aggregatibacter, Cardiobacterium, Eikenella, Kingella) group of endocarditis organisms comes from which source?","choices":["The upper respiratory tract","The genitourinary tract","The skin surface","The lower bowel"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The HACEK (Haemophilus, Aggregatibacter, Cardiobacterium, Eikenella, Kingella) organisms come from the upper respiratory tract. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"In infants, which complications are common because septic emboli are frequent?","choices":["Heart failure from aortic stenosis","Splinter hemorrhages and clubbing","Osteomyelitis, meningitis and pneumonia","Glomerulonephritis and Roth spots"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Septic emboli are common in neonates and infants, producing osteomyelitis, meningitis or pneumonia, and neurologic signs such as seizures, hemiparesis and apnea are frequent. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which is the most common indication for surgery in infective endocarditis?","choices":["Fever on the third hospital day","Heart failure from valvular dysfunction","Any vegetation seen on echocardiogram","A first positive blood culture"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Heart failure due to valvular dysfunction is the most common indication for surgery; others are failure to resolve on appropriate antibiotics, abscess and fungal infection. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Why is fungal infective endocarditis an indication for surgery?","choices":["It is hard to cure with medication alone","It spreads to the pericardium","Antifungals cannot be given intravenously","Fungi do not grow in blood cultures"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Fungal infection is difficult to cure with medications alone, so it joins heart failure, persistent infection and abscess as an indication for surgery. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which description fits Janeway lesions?","choices":["White-centered hemorrhages seen in the retina","Thin dark lines under the proximal nail plate","Painless hemorrhagic macules on palms and soles","Painful nodules on the pads of fingers and toes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Janeway lesions are nontender hemorrhagic lesions on the soles of the feet and palms of the hands, from septic emboli, and are associated with acute infective endocarditis. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"When the presentation suggests acute endocarditis, how quickly should the first cultures be drawn?","choices":["No cultures until antibiotics have begun","Two cultures within the first 1 to 2 hours","One culture at the end of the first day","Three cultures spread over three days"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. If the presentation suggests acute bacterial endocarditis, two cultures are drawn within the first 1-2 hours so that empiric antibiotics are not delayed. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What damages non-valvular endocardium enough for infective endocarditis to take hold there?","choices":["Low blood flow during sleep","Lipid plaque deposition","Pericardial fluid pressure","Blood jets or foreign bodies"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Endocardium damaged by aberrant jets of blood or by foreign bodies becomes a site for infection, alongside the valves and intracardiac devices. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"In a patient at high risk, which procedure does not require endocarditis prophylaxis?","choices":["Tonsillectomy","Root canal","Colonoscopy","Tooth extraction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Gastrointestinal and genitourinary procedures (cystoscopy, upper endoscopy, colonoscopy) do not require endocarditis prophylaxis. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which complete blood count pattern is expected in infective endocarditis?","choices":["Normal white count with polycythemia","Eosinophilia with macrocytic anemia","Leukocytosis with normocytic anemia","Leukopenia with microcytic anemia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The complete blood count with differential shows leukocytosis and anemia of chronic disease with a normal mean corpuscular volume (normocytic). — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Where are splinter hemorrhages found?","choices":["In the retina, with a white center","On the hard palate and conjunctiva","On the pads of the fingers and toes","Under the fingernails and toenails"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Splinter hemorrhages are found under the fingernails and toenails, in the proximal portion of the nail plate, as an embolic phenomenon. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which course is typical of subacute infective endocarditis?","choices":["Low fevers, malaise and weight loss over weeks","High fevers and toxicity within a few days","Sudden collapse with no preceding illness","Painful joints that migrate every few hours"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Subacute infective endocarditis has an indolent course: lower fevers, generalized malaise and weight loss over weeks or months, with slow structural damage, if any. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"On which side of a ventricular septal defect does infective endocarditis develop?","choices":["The high-pressure side","Both sides equally","Neither side; the valves instead","The low-pressure side"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Infective endocarditis can affect the low-pressure side of a ventricular septal defect, where the jet of blood strikes and damages the endocardium; the right side is the low-pressure side. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which organism causes the largest share of health care-associated native valve endocarditis?","choices":["Viridans streptococci","Enterococci","Coagulase-negative staphylococci","Staphylococcus aureus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Staphylococcus aureus causes about 52% of health care-associated native valve endocarditis, against about 28% of community-acquired cases. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Why is clindamycin no longer recommended for endocarditis prophylaxis?","choices":["Risk of Clostridioides difficile","It causes QT prolongation","It misses viridans streptococci","It must be given for weeks"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Clindamycin is no longer recommended for prophylaxis because of the risk of Clostridioides difficile infection. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"In Staphylococcus aureus endocarditis, mortality is highest in which group?","choices":["Native valves, non-injecting","Children with heart disease","Prosthetic valve infection","People who inject drugs"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Staphylococcus aureus mortality is about 50% in prosthetic valve endocarditis, higher than in non-injecting patients (about 30%) and people who inject drugs (about 5%). — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which organism belongs to the fastidious gram-negative group of endocarditis organisms from the upper respiratory tract?","choices":["Streptococcus gallolyticus","Eikenella corrodens","Enterococcus faecalis","Staphylococcus lugdunensis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The HACEK (Haemophilus, Aggregatibacter, Cardiobacterium, Eikenella, Kingella) group is Haemophilus, Aggregatibacter actinomycetemcomitans, Cardiobacterium hominis, Eikenella corrodens and Kingella species, from the upper respiratory tract. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"For how long after congenital heart disease repair with prosthetic material or a device is prophylaxis warranted?","choices":["The first 6 weeks","The first 6 months","The first 5 years","For life"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The first 6 months after congenital heart disease repair with prosthetic material or a device is a high-risk period that warrants prophylaxis. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Without treatment, how quickly does acute infective endocarditis progress to death?","choices":["Within hours","Within weeks","Over several months","Over many years"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. If untreated, acute infective endocarditis progresses to death within weeks, which is why cultures and bactericidal antibiotics cannot wait. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What are Roth spots?","choices":["Thin dark lines under the nails","Painless macules on palms and soles","White-centered retinal hemorrhages","Painful nodules on the finger pads"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Roth spots are white-centered hemorrhages in the retina, from vasculitis affecting the small arteries of the retina. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which single positive blood culture is excluded from the microbiologic minor criterion as a common contaminant?","choices":["Streptococcus gallolyticus","Coagulase-negative staphylococci","Viridans streptococci","Staphylococcus aureus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Single positive cultures for coagulase-negative staphylococci and diphtheroids are excluded because they are common culture contaminants. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which combination of modified Duke criteria establishes definite infective endocarditis?","choices":["Two minor criteria","One major and three minor","One major and one minor","Three minor criteria"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Definite endocarditis is documented by two major criteria, one major and three minor criteria, or five minor criteria. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What causes infective endocarditis?","choices":["Immune complexes alone, with no organism","Coronary plaque rupture with thrombosis","Fibrous scarring of the pericardial sac","Microbes colonizing the endocardium"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Infective endocarditis is caused by invasion and colonization of the endocardial structures by microorganisms, with resulting inflammation and a vegetation. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"By which route are antibiotics for infective endocarditis given?","choices":["Inhaled","Parenterally","Orally","Topically"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Antibiotics should be given parenterally, with hospital admission and close monitoring of labs, renal function and cardiovascular status by a multidisciplinary team. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which organism is a classic cause of subacute infective endocarditis?","choices":["Viridans streptococci","Staphylococcus aureus","Beta-hemolytic streptococci","Candida species"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Subacute endocarditis is caused by viridans streptococci, enterococci, coagulase-negative staphylococci and the HACEK (Haemophilus, Aggregatibacter, Cardiobacterium, Eikenella, Kingella) group. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"When does bronchoscopy call for endocarditis prophylaxis in a high-risk patient?","choices":["Not even when a biopsy is taken","When sedation is planned","When the mucosa is incised, as for biopsy","Every bronchoscopy, whatever is done"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Bronchoscopy needs no prophylaxis unless it involves incision of the respiratory mucosa, such as a biopsy. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 34"}]};
